### 2. The Service Layer (Validation & Execution)
Open your VoucherRequestServiceImpl.java. You need two methods here: one to validate the request synchronously (so the frontend gets an immediate error if they break a rule), and the async method we fixed earlier to actually run the database purge.
```java
    /**
     * Synchronous Validation: Checks rules before allowing deletion.
     */
    @Override
    public void deleteBatchRequest(String batchId, String userId) {
        // 1. Fetch the batch
        VoucherPosting batch = voucherPostingRepository.findById(batchId)
                .orElseThrow(() -> new CustomExceptions.InvalidDataException("Batch not found."));

        // 2. SECURITY CHECK: Only the creator can delete it
        if (!batch.getCreatedBy().equalsIgnoreCase(userId)) {
            log.warn("SECURITY_VIOLATION | User {} attempted to delete batch {} owned by {}", userId, batchId, batch.getCreatedBy());
            throw new IllegalStateException("You do not have permission to delete this batch.");
        }

        // 3. STATE CHECK: Can only delete if it is still PENDING ('P')
        if (!"P".equalsIgnoreCase(batch.getBatchStatus())) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Only Pending batches can be deleted. Current status: " + batch.getBatchStatus());
        }

        // 4. Update status to QUEUED so nobody else can touch it while it's being deleted
        jdbcTemplate.update("UPDATE VOUCHER_POSTING SET BATCH_STATUS = 'QUEUED' WHERE BATCH_ID = ?", batchId);

        // 5. Hand off to the background thread to do the heavy database deletion
        self.executeAsyncBatchCancellation(batchId, userId);
    }

    /**
     * Asynchronous Execution: Runs the Oracle Stored Procedure safely in the background.
     */
    @Async("bulkExecutor")
    public void executeAsyncBatchCancellation(String batchId, String userId) {
        MDC.put("batchId", batchId);
        long start = System.currentTimeMillis();

        try {
            log.info("PURGE_START | Batch: {}", batchId);
            
            // Calls your specific Oracle PL/SQL procedure
            jdbcTemplate.execute("{call PURGE_JOURNAL_BATCH(?, ?)}", (CallableStatementCallback<Object>) cs -> {
                cs.setString(1, batchId);
                cs.setString(2, userId);
                cs.execute();
                return null;
            });
            
            logAudit(userId, "CANCEL_SUCCESS", "DELETE", "Purged Batch " + batchId);
            log.info("BATCH_PURGE_COMPLETE | Batch: {} | Duration: {}ms", batchId, System.currentTimeMillis() - start);
            
        } catch (DataAccessException e) {
            log.error("PURGE_DB_ERROR | Batch: {} | Status: SQL operation failed", batchId, e);
            
            // If the database fails to delete it, revert the status back to 'P' (Pending)
            jdbcTemplate.update("UPDATE VOUCHER_POSTING SET BATCH_STATUS = 'P' WHERE BATCH_ID = ?", batchId);
        } finally {
            // Clean up the Redis Cache so orphaned data doesn't sit in memory
            voucherBulkValidationService.cleanupBulkData(batchId); 
            MDC.clear();
        }
    }

```
### Why this is the correct Backend flow:
 1. **Frontend clicks "Delete"** and calls /JS/voucher/my-requests/by-batch/12345.
 2. **The Controller** intercepts it, verifies the JWT token, and extracts the userId.
 3. **The Service (deleteBatchRequest)** checks the database to ensure the userId matches the CREATED_BY column, and ensures the status is still strictly 'P'.
 4. It flags the batch as 'QUEUED' immediately so the user can't double-click or have a Checker try to approve it simultaneously.
 5. It hands the heavy lifting to executeAsyncBatchCancellation, which triggers your PURGE_JOURNAL_BATCH procedure safely in the background.
